      "text": "\\begin{align*}\n&\\text{First, let's factor out the common terms:} \\\\\n&\\boxed{(10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)} \\\\&= \\boxed{2(5x+1)(2x+1)(5x+5)}.\\end{align*}\n\\text{Now, observe the factors:}\n\\begin{align*}\n\\boxed{(10x+2) = 2(5x+1)} \\text{ and } \\boxed{(10x+6) = 2(5x+3)}.\\end{align*}\t\t\n\\text{So, the expression becomes:}\n\\begin{align*}\n&\\text{ \\em{2}}(5x+1) \\times \\text{ \\em{2}}(5x+3) \\times (5x+5) \\\\\n&\\text{or,} \\\\\n&\\boxed{2^3(5x+1)(5x+3)(5x+5)}.\\end{align*}\t\nFurther simplifying the expression, we get:\n\\begin{align*}\n&\\text{Let } a = 5x+1, b = 5x+3, c = 5x+5, \\\\\n&\\text{then, } \\boxed{2^3ab(c)}.\\end{align*}\t\n\\text{We see that } a, b, \\text{ and } c \\text{ follow an arithmetic progression with a common difference of 2.}\n\n\\text{Since } a, b, \\text{ and } c \\text{ are consecutive terms in an arithmetic sequence with a 2 common difference, } a, b, c \\text{ are in an arithmetic progression.} \n\\text{Each term differs from the previous one by 2. So, we can rewrite the product for values of } a, b, \\text{ and } c\\text{ adding the common difference and subtracting 20.}\n\n\\text{Consider adding 1 to } a: \\\\\n\\em{(5x+2) = a+1}. \\text{Thus, when } x=1, \\text{ we have:} \\\\\na \\times (a+2) \\times (a+4) = (5 \\times 1 + 1)(5 \\times 1 + 3)(5 \\times 1 + 5) = 6 \\times 8 \\times 10, \\text{ which is divisible by } 2^3 \\times 10 = 80.} \\\\\n\\text{Now, subtracting 20 from the product of } a, b, \\text{ and } c \\text{ when } x=1: \\text{}\\boxed{6 \\times 8 \\times (-10)} \\text{ should be a factor of } 2^3 \\times 10 \\times (-20) = -400.} \\\\\n\\text{Now observe } 6 \\times 8 \\times 10 \\div 2^3 \\div 10 \\div 2 = 1. \\text{Now, consider that the terms a,b,c are in arithmetic. } \\\\\n\\text{Hence, solving: $10x=a+b+c \\Rightarrow x=\\frac{a+b+c}{10}$ } \\\\\n\\text{and our final answer is the product of } 2^3ab(c). \\\\\n\\boxed{80}\n\\begin{aligned}\n&2(5x+8)(5x+6)(5x+7) \\\\\n&=2(5x+1)(2x+1)(5x+5)(2x+5) \\\\\n&=2^{3} \\times 15 \\times 7 \\times 10 = 2^{4} \\times 10 \\times 21 \\\\\n&= 2^{4} \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 7 \\times 5 \\\\\n&= 2^{5} \\times 3 \\times 7 \\times 5\n\\end{aligned}\n\\boxed{80}",
