      "text": "We start by factoring the expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\).\n\nFirst, notice that \\(10x+2 = 2(5x+1)\\) and \\(10x+6 = 2(5x+3)\\). So we can rewrite the expression as:\n\\[\n(10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) = (2(5x+1))(2(5x+3))(5x+5).\n\\]\nNext, we can substitute \\(5x+1\\) with \\(y\\), so \\(5x+1 = y\\), \\(5x+3 = y+2\\), and \\(5x+5 = y+2\\). This gives us:\n\\[\n(2y)(2(y+2))(y+2) = 4y(y+2)(y+2) = 4y(y+2)^2.\n\\]\nNow we substitute back \\(y = 5x+1\\):\n\\[\n4(5x+1)(5x+1+2)^2 = 4(5x+1)(5x+3)^2.\n\\]\nSince \\(x\\) is an odd number, \\(5x\\) is also odd. Let \\(5x = 2k+1\\) for some integer \\(k\\). Then \\(5x+1 = 2k+2 = 2(k+1)\\) and \\(5x+3 = 2k+5 = 2(k+2)\\). So the expression becomes:\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 2(k+1) \\cdot (2(k+2))^2 = 4 \\cdot 2(k+1) \\cdot 4(k+2)^2 = 32(k+1)(k+2)^2.\n\\]\nTo find the largest integer that always divides this expression, we need to check the divisibility properties. Notice that \\(2(k+1) \\cdot (k+2)^2\\) is always divisible by \\(4\\) (since it includes the factors 2 and the square of an odd number). Additionally, since \\(k\\) is an integer, \\(k+1\\) and \\(k+2\\) are either both even or both odd. If both are even, the product is divisible by \\(4\\), and if both are odd, the product is divisible by \\(4\\) regardless of the value of \\(k\\). Therefore, the expression is always divisible by \\(16\\).\n\nThus, the largest integer that always divides the expression is \\(\\boxed{320}\\).",
